# Proposed by the executor who built F58 (2026-10-14); for the documenter to adopt or change.
#
# Someone presents Keynote on a shared screen. Mail and Slack are docked out of view, Notes floats on the
# Desktop. Nothing may jump, appear or change size that they did not ask for.
# As the first build shipped it: the whole map of shortcuts, of which nine ship now (22-nine-keys.sim).
set shortcuts all
quiet
open Keynote
open Mail
open Slack
open Notes
dock 101 left
dock 102 right
dock 103 beside 102 bottom
rule com.example.terminal dock bottom
zoom 101
focus 101
expect fills 101
expect hidden 102
expect hidden 103
expect hidden 104

# Hold This View, from the menu.
hold
expect ok
expect held view

# A chat mention: Slack brings itself forward. It stays out of view, and typing goes on in Keynote.
comes-forward 103
expect hidden 103
expect focused 101
expect camera 101

# ⌘Tab to Mail by habit: the same.
focus 102
expect hidden 102
expect focused 101
expect fills 101

# A slip of the fingers on ⌃⌥Space, ⌃⌥↑, ⌃⌥→, or on Arrange Windows.
show-all
expect fail
out
expect fail
neighbor right
expect fail
arrange
expect fail
expect camera 101
expect hidden 102

# A nudge of the title bar, a drag to the screen edge, ⌃⌥F: the one window being shown stays where it is.
drag 101 3 400 cmd
expect fills 101
expect hidden 102
toggle-float 101
expect fail
expect fills 101

# A terminal opens. Its rule would dock it and halve Keynote; it floats instead.
open Terminal
expect floating 105
expect visible 105
expect fills 101

# The talk is over.
release
expect ok
expect held no
expect docked 105
show-all
expect ok
expect visible 102

# A call, not a presentation: Arbor holds still by itself while the meeting's window is open.
zoom 101
focus 101
open Zoom
hold-still-for com.example.zoom Meeting
expect held no
title 106 Zoom Meeting
expect held still
focus 101

# Slack pushes itself forward: no.
comes-forward 103
expect hidden 103
expect focused 101

# The person's own keys and their own ⌘Tab: yes.
neighbor right
expect ok
zoom 101
focus 103
expect visible 103
expect focused 103

# For a demo during the call they hold the view, then let go: the call goes on, and so does holding still.
zoom 101
hold
expect held view
release
expect held still

# The call ends.
close 106
expect held no

# Pausing is the opposite: it brings everything into view, and ends a hold.
zoom 101
hold
pause
expect ok
expect held no
expect visible 102
expect visible 103
resume
expect held no

# Three things that showed an audience everything, found by the product owner on review.
# 1. A slip on a key. ⌃⌥⇧F floats everything: refused. ⌃⌥⎋ pauses, which brings everything into view: held, it only lets go.
show-all
float 105
zoom 101
focus 101
hold
key ctrl+opt+shift+f
expect fail
expect held view
expect hidden 102
key ctrl+opt+escape
expect ok
expect held no
expect fills 101
expect hidden 102
expect hidden 103

# 2. ⌘M on the window being shown, by habit. The wallpaper gives nothing away, and the view is still held.
# (A rule for later, now: settings wait while the view is held.)
rule com.example.console dock beside com.example.mail bottom
hold
set gap 40
expect fail
minimize 101
expect held view
expect hidden 102
expect hidden 103
expect hidden 104
show-all
expect fail
restore 101
expect fills 101
expect hidden 102

# 3. The end of the hold. A console's rule puts it under Mail, which is out of view: that would move the view, so it goes on floating.
open Console
expect floating 107
focus 101
release
expect ok
expect floating 107
expect fills 101
expect hidden 102
expect hidden 103
